      "id": "clm_01M4V613848K8MPBTG9YGQJB8P",
      "statement": "The son of Antiochus III who succeeded him and was killed in 175 BC was Seleucus IV, not Seleucus III.",
      "layer": "evidence",
      "passage": null,
      "classification": "independently_corroborated",
      "reasoning": "The Babylonian king list records Antiochus III's death in SE 125 and the accession of his son Seleucus (IV) for twelve years; Appian names Seleucus, killed through Heliodorus; Jerome calls the king of 11:20 \"Seleucus surnamed Philopator, the son of Antiochus the Great\". Seleucus III (Ceraunus) was Antiochus III's elder brother and predecessor (Jerome on 11:10). Guzik's naming of \"Seleucus III\" at 11:20 therefore appears to be a slip for Seleucus IV; it is recorded as a variant on the prophecy record, not corrected in his name.",
